        <div class="fc-delta-reason">Two structural problems: (1) The dissolution bill currently specifies election date as a range (Sep 8&ndash;Oct 20), not a fixed date &mdash; per F6's ambiguity rule, "passage that sets only a band without a fixed date = AMBIGUOUS-RESOLVE-AS-NO," meaning F6 YES requires both final reading passage AND a legislatively fixed specific date. (2) Times of Israel coalition source (June 16) reports election "likely to be held on the original October 27 date, not earlier"; no second or third readings scheduled; bill described as "unlikely to pass" final readings before July 15 horizon. Netanyahu-Smotrich set aside haredi draft bill, reducing dissolution pressure. Both structural problems independently warrant reduction.</div>
        <dl class="fc-meta-row">
          <dt>Resolution</dt><dd>YES if the Knesset passes the dissolution bill in final (third) reading AND that passage specifies a fixed election date earlier than 2026-10-27, before EOD 2026-07-15.</dd>
          <dt>Sources OK</dt><dd>Two TIER1 (Times of Israel/Reuters/AP/JPost) citing the Knesset plenum vote record.</dd>
          <dt>Ambiguity</dt><dd>Preliminary/first/second readings without final passage do NOT count; passage that sets only a band without a fixed date = AMBIGUOUS-RESOLVE-AS-NO; fixed date on/after 27 Oct = resolve NO.</dd>
        </dl>
        <div class="fc-indicators">
          <span class="fc-indicator state-NOT_OBSERVED">Plenum third-reading vote held with a fixed date inserted</span>
          <span class="fc-indicator state-NOT_OBSERVED">Haredi-coalition agreement on a fixed September or October date</span>
        </div>
