  <!-- ========== 3. 24-48H DELTA ========== -->
  <div class="section" id="delta">
    <h2 class="section-title">
      What Moved (Last 24-48h)
      <span class="meta">Descriptive &middot; banned-verb lint enforced &middot; each paragraph cites a forecast or is marked context-only</span>
    </h2>
    <div class="delta-narrative">
      <p>Pakistan Prime Minister Shehbaz Sharif announced on June 12 that the United States and Iran had reached a "final, agreed upon text" of an MOU through Pakistan-Oman mediation; Trump confirmed on June 14 that both parties had digitally signed the agreement, with a formal ceremony announced for June 19 in Switzerland. The document commits both parties to an "immediate and permanent" cessation of hostilities across all fronts including Lebanon, reopening of Hormuz without tolls, removal of the US naval blockade, and a 60-day window for separate nuclear technical negotiations. Iran's Supreme National Security Council issued a statement via IRNA confirming the agreement; Foreign Minister Araghchi confirmed the two-stage structure publicly. → moved <span class="cite">F1</span>, <span class="cite">F2</span>, <span class="cite">N1</span></p>

      <p>US naval destroyers crossed the Strait of Hormuz on June 15 per CENTCOM statement, with mine-clearance operations actively underway. The IRGC Navy radioed USS Frank E. Petersen with a message stating "This is the last warning" during the MCM operation &mdash; an exchange published by The Hill and Fortune &mdash; while Iran's Foreign Ministry simultaneously notified the UN that non-hostile vessels may transit in coordination with Iranian authorities. The two transmissions arrived within hours of each other from different Iranian command chains, a pattern that constitutes the primary structural risk to the June 19 formal signing and subsequent MCM operations. → moved <span class="cite">F5</span></p>

      <p>Commercial vessel traffic in the strait recorded approximately 29 crossings over the June 10-14 period per AIS tracking data (~6/day average), against a pre-war baseline of approximately 94 daily. BIMCO, the international shipping association, maintained its "highly risky" guidance as of June 15 pending mine clearance confirmation and P&amp;I insurer re-engagement. Shipowners across Japan, South Korea, and Greek operators described their position as awaiting "more details and assurance" before resuming regular transits. Mine-clearing estimates of 40-50 days from June 15 commencement put commercial recovery well beyond the July 2 horizon of F4. → supports <span class="cite">F4</span> without move</p>

      <p>IDF strikes in the Beirut area on June 14 produced reports of Iran preparing a retaliatory response; US officials intervened diplomatically to prevent retaliation ahead of the MOU formal signing timeline. No two-way direct exchange &mdash; Israeli strikes on Iranian targets AND Iranian strikes on Israeli targets &mdash; occurred within a rolling 48h window in the June 10-16 period. The June 7-8 bilateral exchange documented by CNN preceded the June 10 window start and does not qualify under F3's resolution criteria. The bilateral ceasefire commitment in the June 14 MOU applies to direct Iran-Israel hostilities, though Hezbollah, which is not an MOU signatory, retains independent operational tempo. → moved <span class="cite">F3</span></p>

      <p>Israel's Knesset dissolution bill &mdash; which passed its first reading 106-0 on June 2 &mdash; has not advanced to second or third reading as of June 16. A coalition source cited by Times of Israel said the election is "likely to be held on the original October 27 date, not earlier," with parliamentary recess expected to start July 16. Netanyahu and Finance Minister Smotrich agreed to set aside the haredi draft exemption bill in exchange for budgetary concessions from ultra-Orthodox coalition partners, reducing one of the primary dissolution pressure vectors. The dissolution bill's current text specifies an election date range (September 8 to October 20), not a fixed date; under F6's ambiguity rule, passage with a range only resolves AMBIGUOUS-AS-NO. → moved <span class="cite">F6</span></p>
    </div>
  </div>

  <!-- ========== 4. SCENARIO MAP ========== -->
  <div class="section" id="scenario-map">
    <h2 class="section-title">
      Scenario Map
      <span class="meta">Computed from Forecast Board &middot; 1 sentence per scenario</span>
    </h2>
    <div class="scenario-map">
      <div class="prob-bar-container">
        <div class="prob-bar prob-escalation" style="width:25%;">Escalation 25%</div>
        <div class="prob-bar prob-status" style="width:35%;">Protracted 35%</div>
        <div class="prob-bar prob-deescalation" style="width:40%;">De-esc 40%</div>
      </div>
      <p class="scenario-line"><strong class="label-esc">Escalation path (25%):</strong> If the June 19 signing collapses or Israel conducts a spoiler strike before the ceremony, the ceasefire trajectory reverses and a new bilateral exchange window opens before June 24 &mdash; the key falsifier for this scenario is an IRGC incident with MCM vessels or renewed IDF action on Iranian territory.</p>
      <p class="scenario-line"><strong class="label-prot">Protracted path (35%):</strong> If the signing is delayed past June 22 but the MOU framework nominally holds, mine-clearing begins late, transit recovery lags the July 2 threshold, and the diplomatic track enters a fragile holding pattern without a resolved endpoint through July.</p>
      <p class="scenario-line"><strong class="label-desc">De-escalation path (40%):</strong> If the June 19 ceremony proceeds as confirmed and MCM operations continue on schedule, Hormuz begins staged reopening under coalition oversight and the direct-exchange risk falls further below 30% through the remainder of June, with nuclear talks commencing within the MOU's 60-day window.</p>
    </div>
  </div>

  <!-- ========== 5. INSIDE IRAN ========== -->
  <div class="section" id="inside-iran">
    <h2 class="section-title">
      Inside Iran
      <span class="meta">State-media decode &middot; 200 words &middot; verb-ban + forecast-cite rule applies</span>
    </h2>
    <div class="inside-iran">
      <div class="iran-media-badges">
        <span class="iran-media-badge">IRNA</span>
        <span class="iran-media-badge">IRIB</span>
        <span class="iran-media-badge">Press TV</span>
        <span class="iran-media-badge">Fars</span>
        <span class="iran-media-badge">Tasnim</span>
        <span class="iran-media-badge">Kayhan</span>
      </div>
      <p>Through June 13-16, IRNA and Tasnim published a tightly controlled narrative: MFA spokesman Nasser Kanaani characterized the diplomatic process as Iran exercising its sovereign right to determine engagement terms, without naming the June 12-14 mediated session explicitly. The SNSC statement confirmed the MOU but framed it as Iran's own initiative rather than a US-brokered outcome. Araghchi emphasized the two-stage structure publicly, with Stage Two nuclear negotiations described as a separate track that Iran has not yet entered. → <span class="context-only">context only</span></p>

      <p>Press TV broadcast extended commentary on the mine-clearing question, emphasizing that Iran retains veto authority over the timeline of MCM operations regardless of treaty text. This framing runs counter to the deal's reported terms, which stipulate a US-led coalition operation beginning June 19. The gap between civilian MFA track (UN notification permitting transit) and IRGC field track (radio warning to USS Frank E. Petersen) was not acknowledged by any state-media outlet through June 16 &mdash; IRIB presented both transmissions as consistent Iranian policy. → <span class="context-only">context only</span></p>

      <p>IRIB's internal Farsi channel carried no reference to the June 12-14 framework as a concession to the United States, instead using the term "mutual arrangement" (&#x062A;&#x0648;&#x0627;&#x0641;&#x0642; &#x0645;&#x062A;&#x0642;&#x0627;&#x0628;&#x0644;). Kayhan published editorials framing the MOU as conditional on Israeli withdrawal from Lebanon, a framing not reflected in TIER1 reports of the deal's actual terms. Fars News ran three items on the Knesset dissolution bill, framing Netanyahu's coalition trajectory as evidence of the "Zionist regime's structural fragility" &mdash; standard domestic-audience framing unconnected to any Iranian military or diplomatic action. → <span class="context-only">context only</span></p>
    </div>
  </div>

